course: dictionary
lesson: 12080
title: Pieces of longer words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words you meet are not whole words at all. «Clon» is a piece of a longer word, cut off in the middle or at the start. You find it inside «clone» and «cyclone».
explain: When you see a piece like this, look at the whole word. The rest of the word helps you work out what the piece is part of. «Clone» is an exact copy of a living thing. «Cyclone» is a huge storm.
explain: In Ireland, «Clon» at the start of a place name often comes from an old Irish word, cluain, which means meadow. «Clonmel» and «Clontarf» are two examples. Their names begin with clon, but the rest of each name matters too.
fact: A fragment is a piece of a word, not a whole word on its own.
fact: «Clon» is usually a piece of a longer word such as «clone» or «cyclone».
fact: «Clone» means an exact copy of a living thing.
fact: «Cyclone» means a huge storm.
fact: In Irish place names, «Clon-» comes from «cluain», which means meadow.
exercise: Which longer word has the piece clon in the middle? | Clip | Cyan | *Cyclone | Clone
exercise: «Clonmel is a lovely town in Ireland.» The start of Clonmel comes from an old Irish word. What does it mean? | Sea | *Meadow | Mountain
exercise: «The cyclone blew down trees all night.» What is a cyclone? | A kind of bird | An exact copy of a living thing | *A huge storm | A small town in Ireland
test: Sam says «clon» is a word you can say on its own. Is he right? | *No, it is a piece of a longer word | Yes, it means a storm | Yes, it means a copy | Only in Ireland
test: «The clone looked just like its twin.» What does clone mean? | A huge storm | A town in Ireland | *An exact copy of a living thing | A meadow
exam: Which place name starts with clon, from the Irish word cluain meaning meadow? | Cork | Belfast | Galway | *Clontarf
